{
"content_id": "14411",
"reference_id": 13579,
"version": "1.0.4",
"title": "3 John 1:3",
"media_type": "Text",
"index_reference": "64001003",
"language": "eng",
"review_level": "Professional",
"content": "<ul><li><p>The traveling teachers (literally <em>brothers</em>) were probably John&apos;s messengers. They traveled between churches, teaching the good news about Jesus and encouraging Christians (see <a href=\"https://ref.ly/3John1:5\">1:5</a>, <a href=\"https://ref.ly/3John1:10\">10</a>). </p></li><li><p><span><em>your devotion to the truth</em></span>: Gaius remained faithful to the message about Jesus Christ that the apostles taught. The false teachers denied that Jesus truly came in the flesh or that his death was a real sacrifice (compare <a href=\"https://ref.ly/1John4:1-1John4:3\">1&nbsp;John 4:1–3</a>).</p></li></ul>",
"associations": {
"passage": [
{
"start_ref": "64001003",
"start_ref_usfm": "3JN 1:3",
"end_ref": "64001003",
"end_ref_usfm": "3JN 1:3"
}
],
